    "result_text": "I implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py` and added a test for it. Both tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed). The test uses a fake API object, so nothing was actually uploaded to the Hub.\n\n**The usual large-folder function doesn't exist in the pinned version.** The project pins `huggingface-hub==2.0.0`, which has no `upload_large_folder`. In 2.0 that job is done by the ordinary `upload_folder`. When the `hf_xet` package is installed, it splits large folders into several commits to stay under server limits. If an upload dies partway, running the same call again skips files already committed and doesn't re-send data already uploaded. `hf_xet` 1.6.0 is already in `.venv`, so this path is active and no new dependency is needed.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `create_repo(repo_id, repo_type=\"dataset\", private=True, exist_ok=True)` creates the private dataset repo, or leaves an existing one as it is and uploads into it.\n- `upload_folder(...)` uploads to the repo root with `ignore_patterns=[\"*.tmp\", \"wandb/\"]`. In this library `*` also matches across folders, so `*.tmp` skips temp files at any depth and `wandb/` skips everything under the top-level `wandb/` folder.\n- Like `publish_summary`, it takes an optional `api` argument so tests can pass in a fake.\n\n**Things to know**\n- **Keep `hf_xet` installed:** if it's missing, or `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET` is set, `upload_folder` falls back to a single commit. That can fail on folders this big and can't resume.\n- **Commit messages:** a large run will show up as several commits: \"Upload run <name>\", then \"(part N)\" versions of it.\n- **Nested `wandb/` folders:** only the top-level `wandb/` is skipped. If `wandb/` folders can appear deeper in the run folder, add `\"*/wandb/*\"` to the ignore patterns."
  },
